{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes Mister B has free evenings when he doesn't know what to do. Fortunately, Mister B found a new game, where the player can play against aliens.All characters in this game are lowercase English letters. There are two players: Mister B and his competitor.Initially the players have a string s consisting of the first a English letters in alphabetical order (for example, if a\u2009=\u20095, then s equals to \"abcde\").The players take turns appending letters to string s. Mister B moves first.Mister B must append exactly b letters on each his move. He can arbitrary choose these letters. His opponent adds exactly a letters on each move.Mister B quickly understood that his opponent was just a computer that used a simple algorithm. The computer on each turn considers the suffix of string s of length a and generates a string t of length a such that all letters in the string t are distinct and don't appear in the considered suffix. From multiple variants of t lexicographically minimal is chosen (if a\u2009=\u20094 and the suffix is \"bfdd\", the computer chooses string t equal to \"aceg\"). After that the chosen string t is appended to the end of s.Mister B soon found the game boring and came up with the following question: what can be the minimum possible number of different letters in string s on the segment between positions l and r, inclusive. Letters of string s are numerated starting from 1.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 8 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Div2_421C {\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(reader.readLine());\n        int a = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());\n        int b = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());\n        int l = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken()) - 1;\n        int r = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());\n\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < a; i++) {\n            sb.append((char) ('a' + i));\n        }\n        char lastChar = sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1);\n        for (int i = 0; i < b; i++) {\n            sb.append(lastChar);\n        }\n\n        Set<Integer> used = new TreeSet<>();\n        int startIdx = sb.length() - a;\n        for (int i = startIdx; i < sb.length(); i++) {\n            used.add(sb.charAt(i) - 'a');\n        }\n\n        int nextChar = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < a; i++) {\n            while (used.contains(nextChar)) {\n                nextChar++;\n            }\n            sb.append((char) ('a' + nextChar));\n            nextChar++;\n        }\n\n        lastChar = sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1);\n        for (int i = 0; i < b; i++) {\n            sb.append(lastChar);\n        }\n\n        int totalLen = sb.length();\n        used.clear();\n\n        if (r - l >= totalLen) {\n            for (int i = 0; i < totalLen; i++) {\n                used.add(sb.charAt(i) - 'a');\n            }\n        } else {\n            for (int i = l; i < r; i = (i + 1) % totalLen) {\n                used.add(sb.charAt(i) - 'a');\n                if (i == (r - 1) % totalLen && r % totalLen <= l) break;\n            }\n            // Handle wrap-around case properly\n            if (r > totalLen) {\n                int wrappedR = r % totalLen;\n                for (int i = 0; i < wrappedR; i++) {\n                    used.add(sb.charAt(i) - 'a');\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        System.out.println(used.size());\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Div2_421C {\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(reader.readLine());\n        int a = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());\n        int b = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());\n        int l = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken()) - 1;\n        int r = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());\n\n        // Build initial string: first a letters in alphabetical order\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < a; i++) {\n            sb.append((char) ('a' + i));\n        }\n\n        // Simulate the game until we have enough characters to cover position r\n        // We need at least r characters in our string\n        while (sb.length() < r) {\n            // Mister B's turn: append b letters (he chooses optimally to minimize distinct letters)\n            // To minimize distinct letters, Mister B should append the same letter repeatedly\n            // The best choice is to use the last letter of current string\n            char lastChar = sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1);\n            for (int i = 0; i < b; i++) {\n                sb.append(lastChar);\n            }\n\n            // Computer's turn: append a letters\n            // Computer considers the last a characters and finds the lexicographically smallest\n            // string with distinct letters not in that suffix\n            Set<Integer> usedInSuffix = new HashSet<>();\n            int startIdx = Math.max(0, sb.length() - a);\n            for (int i = startIdx; i < sb.length(); i++) {\n                usedInSuffix.add(sb.charAt(i) - 'a');\n            }\n\n            int nextChar = 0;\n            for (int i = 0; i < a; i++) {\n                while (usedInSuffix.contains(nextChar)) {\n                    nextChar++;\n                }\n                sb.append((char) ('a' + nextChar));\n                usedInSuffix.add(nextChar);\n                nextChar++;\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Count distinct letters in range [l, r] (0-indexed, inclusive)\n        Set<Integer> used = new HashSet<>();\n        for (int i = l; i < r; i++) {\n            used.add(sb.charAt(i) - 'a');\n        }\n\n        System.out.println(used.size());\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-1.0728830375228426e-06, -6.496695277746767e-05, -2.861018856492592e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -4.494089080253616e-05, -2.062299427052494e-05, -1.0132738680113107e-05, -3.933898824470816e-06, -8.177422569133341e-05, -7.986990567587782e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -2.6225699912174605e-05, -5.364403477869928e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -8.95221673999913e-05, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -8.344646857949556e-07, -7.033323527139146e-06, -3.576278118089249e-07, -1.4305104514278355e-06, -0.0008058876264840364, -7.152555099310121e-07, -4.768360213347478e-06, -7.152555099310121e-07, -8.344646857949556e-07, -2.2291887944447808e-05, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -7.390948667307384e-06, -1.4781842764932662e-05, -1.2159273865108844e-05, -4.911301948595792e-05, -1.168244216387393e-05, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.6689286894688848e-0